-- Q4561: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Required in all group audit engagements regardless of circumstances","Not permitted under any circumstances per auditing standards","Permitted for non-issuer audits when conditions are met, dividing responsibility","Required only when the component auditor is a foreign subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): For non-issuer audits, the group engagement partner may make reference to a component auditor in the group audit report, which divides responsibility between the group and component auditors. AU-C 600 permits this when the component auditor is independent and complies with relevant standards. This option is not available for issuer audits under PCAOB standards.
Wrong (A): Reference to a component auditor is permitted, not required. The group auditor may choose to make or not make reference.
Wrong (B): Reference is permitted for non-issuer audits under AU-C 600, so it is not prohibited under all circumstances.
Wrong (D): The decision to make reference is not limited to foreign subsidiaries; it applies to any component audit meeting the required conditions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4561;

-- Q4562: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion without further evidence about the service organization","Require the user entity to terminate the service organization relationship immediately","Disclaim an opinion without first attempting to obtain alternative evidence","Obtain a SOC report, perform direct tests, or engage another auditor for procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 402, when the user auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence from the user entity alone, the auditor may obtain and evaluate a SOC 1 report, contact the service organization to request specific procedures, or engage another auditor to perform procedures at the service organization. These alternatives ensure adequate evidence is gathered.
Wrong (A): Issuing an unmodified opinion without sufficient evidence would violate AU-C 402 requirements for obtaining adequate audit evidence.
Wrong (B): The auditor has no authority to require termination of a business relationship between the entity and its service organization.
Wrong (C): The auditor must first attempt alternative procedures before considering a scope limitation or disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4562;

-- Q4563: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The specialist''s fee arrangement and billing terms with the audit firm","Whether the specialist uses the same audit software as the engagement team","The specialist''s political affiliations and personal voting record","Whether the specialist has entity relationships that could impair objectivity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620, the auditor must evaluate whether the specialist has any relationship with the entity—such as financial interests, business relationships, or employment ties—that could compromise objectivity. If the specialist is management''s specialist, objectivity concerns are particularly important because the specialist may be influenced by management''s interests.
Wrong (A): Fee arrangements with the audit firm relate to the auditor''s own independence, not the specialist''s objectivity regarding the entity.
Wrong (B): Software compatibility is a practical consideration but has no bearing on the specialist''s objectivity.
Wrong (C): Political affiliations are not relevant to evaluating a specialist''s professional objectivity regarding the entity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4563;

-- Q4567: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recalculation of recorded account balances and financial totals","Observation of personnel duties and inspection of authorization signatures","Sending confirmation letters to the entity''s major customers","Performing analytical procedures on disbursement account balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Observation and inspection are the most effective procedures for testing segregation of duties under AU-C 330. The auditor can observe that different individuals perform incompatible functions and inspect authorization signatures, access logs, and approvals to verify the control operates as designed.
Wrong (A): Recalculation tests mathematical accuracy of amounts, not whether duties are properly segregated among personnel.
Wrong (C): Confirmations verify account balances with third parties and do not test internal segregation of duties.
Wrong (D): Analytical procedures identify unusual relationships in financial data but cannot confirm that specific duties are performed by separate individuals.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4567;

-- Q4568: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only one occurrence because the control is considered well-designed","Most or all occurrences given the small population of twelve instances","Only the single most recent occurrence before the reporting date","Only the occurrences performed during the final quarter of the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, for controls that operate infrequently such as a monthly reconciliation, the auditor typically tests each occurrence or most occurrences because the small population does not lend itself to sampling. Testing all twelve instances provides the strongest evidence of consistent operating effectiveness throughout the period.
Wrong (A): Testing only one occurrence of a manual control provides insufficient evidence of consistent operation across the full period.
Wrong (C): The most recent occurrence alone does not demonstrate that the control operated effectively during prior months.
Wrong (D): Limiting testing to year-end ignores nine months of activity and cannot support reliance for the entire audit period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4568;

-- Q4569: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Tests two different controls simultaneously within the same department","Tests both control effectiveness and substantive account balance evidence","Tests the same control at two geographically separate locations","Tests the identical control at two different points during the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A dual-purpose test simultaneously serves as both a test of controls and a substantive test of details, as described in AU-C 330. For example, examining a sales invoice for proper credit approval tests the control while verifying the recorded amount provides substantive evidence. This approach efficiently addresses both objectives in a single procedure.
Wrong (A): Testing two different controls simultaneously is not a dual-purpose test; dual-purpose refers to combining control and substantive objectives.
Wrong (C): Testing a control at multiple locations is geographic expansion of a control test, not a dual-purpose test.
Wrong (D): Testing the same control at different times is testing over a period, not combining control and substantive objectives.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4569;

-- Q4572: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor observing management perform the control procedure","The auditor independently executing the control to verify expected results","Management repeating the control at the auditor''s specific request","The auditor recalculating year-end financial statement account balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Reperformance involves the auditor independently executing the same procedure or control that entity personnel performed, to determine whether it produces correct results. Under AU-C 330, this provides direct evidence of operating effectiveness—for example, independently reconciling a bank statement to verify management''s reconciliation control.
Wrong (A): Observing management perform a control is the observation technique, which is a separate audit procedure from reperformance.
Wrong (C): Having management repeat the control is not reperformance; the auditor must independently execute the procedure.
Wrong (D): Recalculating financial statement balances is a substantive procedure, not a test of controls through reperformance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4572;

-- Q4573: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test the control once or a few times plus test supporting IT general controls","Test every single transaction that was processed through the automated system","Test the control the same number of times as a comparable manual control","Skip testing automated controls because they are considered inherently reliable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, automated controls are inherently consistent—once programmed correctly, they perform identically every time. The auditor typically tests the control once or a small number of times to verify correct operation, combined with testing IT general controls (program change controls, access controls) that ensure the automated control has not been altered.
Wrong (B): Testing every transaction is unnecessary and impractical because automated controls produce identical results each time they execute.
Wrong (C): Automated controls do not require the same sample sizes as manual controls due to their inherent consistency of execution.
Wrong (D): Automated controls must still be tested; inherent consistency does not eliminate the need to verify they are correctly programmed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4573;

-- Q4574: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assess the impact on risk and determine what additional procedures are needed","Ignore the control failure if it occurred early in the audit period","Immediately issue an adverse opinion on the financial statements","Continue to rely on the control for the entire reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, when a control failure is discovered, the auditor must assess its impact on the risk assessment and planned audit procedures. The auditor determines the nature and extent of additional substantive procedures needed for the period when the control was not operating effectively, and considers whether the failure indicates a systemic problem.
Wrong (B): A control failure cannot be ignored based on timing alone; the auditor must assess its impact regardless of when it occurred.
Wrong (C): A control failure does not automatically warrant an adverse opinion; the auditor first assesses whether it leads to a material misstatement.
Wrong (D): Continuing full reliance on a control known to have failed during the period would result in insufficient audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4574;

-- Q4576: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Support a lower control risk assessment, allowing reduced substantive testing","Ensure the entity is operating profitably during the audit period","Prepare the entity''s financial statements for external distribution","Determine the appropriate interest rate the entity uses for debt"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, the primary reason for testing controls is to support a lower assessed level of control risk, which allows the auditor to reduce the nature, timing, or extent of substantive procedures. If controls are effective, less substantive testing is needed because the auditor has greater confidence that transactions are properly recorded.
Wrong (B): Evaluating profitability is not a purpose of testing controls; controls relate to financial reporting reliability, not business performance.
Wrong (C): Preparing financial statements is management''s responsibility, not an objective of the auditor''s control testing.
Wrong (D): Determining interest rates for debt is a management decision unrelated to the auditor''s assessment of internal controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4576;

-- Q4577: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test all 250 occurrences to ensure complete coverage of the population","Test only one occurrence because the control operates every single day","Select a sample of occurrences sufficient for concluding on effectiveness","Test only the occurrences processed during the month of December"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, for controls that operate frequently such as daily, the auditor uses sampling to select a sufficient number of occurrences for testing. The sample size depends on the desired level of assurance, the expected deviation rate, and the planned reliance on the control. Auditing standards provide guidance on appropriate sample sizes.
Wrong (A): Testing all 250 occurrences is unnecessary and inefficient; sampling is appropriate for high-frequency controls.
Wrong (B): Testing only one occurrence of a manual daily control does not provide adequate evidence of consistent operating effectiveness.
Wrong (D): Testing only December occurrences ignores eleven months of activity and cannot support reliance for the full audit period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4577;

-- Q4578: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They directly process and record the entity''s financial transactions","They are specifically required by federal and state tax regulations","They support effective functioning of automated controls and data integrity","They determine whether the entity is operating at a profitable level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315, IT general controls—such as access controls, program change controls, and computer operations controls—support the reliable functioning of automated application controls and the integrity of data processed by IT systems. Without effective ITGCs, automated controls may not be trustworthy because unauthorized changes could compromise their effectiveness.
Wrong (A): ITGCs do not directly process financial transactions; that function belongs to application controls within specific business processes.
Wrong (B): ITGCs are driven by internal control and audit standards, not tax regulations.
Wrong (D): ITGCs relate to IT system reliability and data integrity, not to evaluating the entity''s profitability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4578;

-- Q4579: Tests of Controls [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately issue a qualified opinion on the financial statements","Ignore the deviation if only a single instance was identified","Evaluate whether it is isolated or systemic and assess the control risk impact","Expand the sample to cover one hundred percent of the entire population"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, a deviation in a test of controls requires the auditor to evaluate whether it represents an isolated occurrence or indicates a systemic weakness. The auditor should consider the nature and cause of the deviation, whether the deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate, and how it affects the assessed level of control risk and planned reliance.
Wrong (A): A single deviation does not automatically warrant a qualified opinion; the auditor must first assess its significance and impact.
Wrong (B): Even a single deviation must be evaluated for its cause and potential implications for the control environment.
Wrong (D): Expanding to 100% testing is impractical and unnecessary; the auditor should evaluate the deviation and adjust the risk assessment accordingly.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4579;

-- Q4581: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Must formally justify this decision to the entity''s audit committee","Must still test controls for all risks identified as significant","Cannot issue an unmodified opinion using a substantive-only approach","Assesses control risk at maximum and designs substantive procedures accordingly"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, when the auditor elects not to test controls, control risk is effectively assessed at the maximum level. The auditor then designs substantive procedures with sufficient nature, timing, and extent to address the higher assessed risks at the assertion level. This fully substantive approach is common for smaller entities or where control testing would be inefficient.
Wrong (A): While communication with those charged with governance may occur, formal justification to the audit committee is not required for this decision.
Wrong (B): AU-C 330 permits a substantive-only approach; the auditor is not required to test controls for every significant risk.
Wrong (C): An unmodified opinion can be issued if substantive procedures provide sufficient appropriate evidence regardless of control reliance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4581;

-- Q4582: Tests of Controls [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Has been consistently applied by personnel throughout the reporting period","Generates sufficient documentation to support the audit engagement file","Requires additional personnel or resources in order to operate effectively","Is suitably designed to prevent or detect material misstatements at assertions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315, evaluating design effectiveness means assessing whether the control, if operating as designed, would effectively prevent or detect and correct material misstatements at the relevant assertion level. Design effectiveness is a prerequisite evaluation—distinct from operating effectiveness, which tests whether the control actually functioned as designed throughout the period.
Wrong (A): Consistent application throughout the period relates to operating effectiveness, not design effectiveness.
Wrong (B): Whether a control generates audit documentation is not the focus of design effectiveness evaluation.
Wrong (C): Staffing requirements are an implementation consideration, not a measure of whether the control is suitably designed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4582;

-- Q4583: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["At least once every five years under a rotational testing cycle","At least every other year when no changes have been identified","In each audit period, though extent may be reduced via rotation","Only when the entity specifically requests additional control testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, if the auditor plans to rely on controls, some controls must be tested in each audit period. The auditor may use a rotational approach where different controls are tested each year, but a control should not go untested for more than two consecutive periods. All controls relied upon should be tested at least once in every three-year rotation.
Wrong (A): A five-year gap between tests is too long; AU-C 330 requires testing within a three-year rotation at minimum.
Wrong (B): Every other year is not the standard; controls must be tested each period, though individual controls may rotate.
Wrong (D): The testing requirement is driven by auditing standards, not by client request.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4583;

-- Q4584: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Financial statements for errors in presentation and disclosure format","Customer correspondence for complaints about products or services","Bank statements for unusual deposit patterns or unexplained amounts","Evidence the control was performed such as approvals and signatures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, inspection of documents as a test of controls involves examining evidence that the control was performed—such as approval signatures on purchase orders, initials on reconciliations, exception reports showing items investigated, and other markers indicating the control activity was carried out as designed.
Wrong (A): Inspecting financial statements for presentation errors is a substantive procedure, not a test of controls over purchase approvals.
Wrong (B): Reviewing customer correspondence addresses customer satisfaction, not the operating effectiveness of purchase approval controls.
Wrong (C): Examining bank statements for unusual deposits is a substantive procedure related to cash, not a test of the purchase approval control.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4584;

-- Q4585: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No additional testing is needed because IT controls remain inherently consistent","The auditor should defer all control testing until the following audit period","The auditor should only test controls that were implemented after the change","The auditor may need to test controls both before and after the change"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, when significant IT system changes occur during the audit period, the auditor may need to test controls in place before the change for the pre-change portion and controls implemented after the change for the remaining period. This ensures effective controls were operating throughout the entire period of intended reliance.
Wrong (A): System changes can alter how controls operate, so the auditor cannot assume consistency without testing both environments.
Wrong (B): Deferring all control testing to the next period would leave the current audit without evidence of control effectiveness.
Wrong (C): Testing only post-change controls ignores the portion of the period when the prior system was in use.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4585;

-- Q4586: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["During both the planning and overall review phases of the audit","Only during the substantive testing phase of the engagement","Only during the final overall review phase before issuing the report","Only during the initial planning phase of the audit engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 520, analytical procedures are required during two phases: the planning phase, to identify areas of potential misstatement and assist in risk assessment, and the overall review phase near the end of the audit, to assess whether the financial statements are consistent with the auditor''s understanding of the entity. Substantive analytical procedures are optional but commonly used.
Wrong (B): Substantive analytical procedures during testing are permitted but not required; the mandatory phases are planning and overall review.
Wrong (C): The final review phase is only one of the two required phases; planning is also mandatory.
Wrong (D): The planning phase is only one of the two required phases; the overall review is also mandatory.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4586;

-- Q4587: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provide substantive audit evidence about specific account balances","Identify unusual relationships indicating increased misstatement risk","Confirm the accuracy of the prior year''s audited financial statements","Test the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, during planning, analytical procedures help the auditor identify unusual or unexpected relationships, trends, or fluctuations that may indicate areas of increased risk of material misstatement. These procedures assist in understanding the entity''s business and focus attention on areas requiring additional audit procedures.
Wrong (A): Providing substantive evidence is the purpose of substantive analytical procedures during fieldwork, not planning analytics.
Wrong (C): Planning analytics compare preliminary data to expectations; they do not confirm prior-year accuracy.
Wrong (D): Testing operating effectiveness of controls is performed through tests of controls, not planning analytical procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4587;

-- Q4590: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s explanation without performing further investigation","Investigate the difference through inquiry and additional audit procedures","Immediately propose adjusting entries to correct the financial statements","Ignore the difference if the recorded amount falls below overall materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, when a substantive analytical procedure identifies a significant unexpected difference, the auditor must investigate by inquiring of management and obtaining corroborating evidence. The auditor assesses whether the difference is due to a misstatement or a legitimate business factor, and may need to perform additional tests of details.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s explanation without corroboration fails to meet the auditor''s obligation to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence.
Wrong (C): The auditor investigates and evaluates the cause before proposing any adjustments; immediate adjustment without investigation is inappropriate.
Wrong (D): A difference identified through substantive analytics requires investigation regardless of the amount, as it may indicate a misstatement pattern.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4590;

-- Q4591: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace the need for substantive testing on all immaterial accounts","Help form an overall conclusion on financial statement consistency","Detect every remaining misstatement contained in the financial statements","Test the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, the final analytical review is performed near the end of the audit to help the auditor form an overall conclusion about whether the financial statements as a whole are consistent with the auditor''s understanding of the entity and its environment. It serves as a reasonableness check that may identify previously unrecognized risks.
Wrong (A): The overall review does not replace substantive testing; it is a high-level assessment performed after substantive procedures are complete.
Wrong (C): The overall review is not designed to detect every misstatement; it identifies broad inconsistencies warranting further investigation.
Wrong (D): The final analytical review assesses financial statement consistency, not the operating effectiveness of controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4591;

-- Q4592: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s general ledger trial balance and supporting journal entries","Non-financial data such as square footage to independently estimate revenue","Bank statements showing cash receipts and deposit transaction details","Accounts receivable confirmation replies received from the entity''s customers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, analytical procedures can incorporate non-financial data to develop expectations. For example, an auditor might use square footage multiplied by average rental rates to estimate rental revenue, or use production capacity and operating hours to estimate costs. Non-financial data can provide highly persuasive analytical evidence.
Wrong (A): The general ledger is a financial data source internal to the accounting system, not a non-financial data source.
Wrong (C): Bank statements are financial documents that record monetary transactions, not non-financial operational data.
Wrong (D): Confirmation replies are a financial audit procedure verifying recorded monetary balances, not non-financial data.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4592;

-- Q4593: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing financial data across multiple periods to identify unusual fluctuations","Confirming recorded account balances with independent third-party sources","Inspecting supporting documentation for individually selected transactions","Testing the design and implementation effectiveness of internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Trend analysis is a type of analytical procedure under AU-C 520 that involves comparing financial data across multiple periods to identify patterns, trends, or unusual fluctuations. It helps the auditor understand performance trajectory and may reveal unexpected changes warranting investigation, such as sudden expense spikes or revenue declines.
Wrong (B): Confirming balances with third parties is the confirmation procedure, a separate audit technique from analytical procedures.
Wrong (C): Inspecting supporting documentation for individual transactions is a test of details, not an analytical procedure.
Wrong (D): Evaluating internal control design is part of risk assessment procedures, not a form of analytical procedure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4593;

-- Q4594: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The ratios are compared only to the entity''s own prior-year ratios","The ratios are compared to industry averages, budgets, or other benchmarks","The ratios are calculated only for the balance sheet account categories","The ratios are provided by management without any independent verification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, ratio analysis is most useful when calculated ratios are compared to meaningful benchmarks such as industry averages, prior-period ratios, budgeted amounts, or non-financial operational data. These comparisons help the auditor identify deviations from expected relationships and assess whether further investigation is needed.
Wrong (A): Comparing only to the entity''s own prior-year ratios limits the analysis; external and budgeted benchmarks provide broader context.
Wrong (C): Ratio analysis applies to both balance sheet and income statement accounts and is not restricted to one category.
Wrong (D): Relying on management-provided ratios without independent verification undermines the objectivity of the analytical procedure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4594;

-- Q4595: Analytical Procedures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor''s materiality assessment and desired level of assurance","Management''s stated preference for the acceptable tolerance level","A fixed percentage specifically established by the auditing standards","The total amount of the audit engagement fee for the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 520, the threshold for acceptable difference (sometimes called precision) is based on the auditor''s assessment of materiality and the desired level of assurance from the analytical procedure. A narrower threshold provides more assurance, and any difference exceeding the threshold is investigated as a potential misstatement.
Wrong (B): Management''s preference does not determine the threshold; it is set by the auditor using professional judgment based on materiality.
Wrong (C): Auditing standards do not prescribe a fixed percentage; the threshold depends on the auditor''s professional judgment and circumstances.
Wrong (D): The audit fee has no bearing on the acceptable threshold for differences in substantive analytical procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4595;

-- Q4596: Analytical Procedures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Simply comparing current-year balances to the prior-year recorded amounts","Interviewing management about their expectations for financial results","A statistical technique using independent variables to predict a dependent variable","Testing a sample of individual transactions for proper authorization documentation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Regression analysis is a sophisticated analytical procedure under AU-C 520 that uses statistical techniques to model the relationship between a dependent variable (e.g., payroll expense) and one or more independent variables (e.g., headcount, wage rates). It quantifies the strength of the relationship and develops a more precise expectation than simple comparisons.
Wrong (A): Simple year-over-year comparison is trend analysis, a less sophisticated technique that does not use statistical modeling.
Wrong (B): Interviewing management is the inquiry procedure, not a statistical analytical technique.
Wrong (D): Testing transactions for authorization is a test of controls or test of details, not an analytical procedure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4596;

-- Q4598: Analytical Procedures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Combining all available data into a single aggregate total for comparison","Using financial data from the prior year as the sole basis for analysis","Performing analytical procedures only on the year-end closing balances","Breaking data into smaller homogeneous components to improve precision"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 520, disaggregating data means breaking it into smaller, more homogeneous subsets—such as by location, product line, or month—rather than analyzing aggregate totals. Disaggregation improves the precision of the expectation because relationships within homogeneous groups are more predictable, making it easier to detect misstatements.
Wrong (A): Combining data into a single total is aggregation, the opposite of disaggregation, which reduces the precision of the analysis.
Wrong (B): Using only prior-year data describes the benchmark choice, not the technique of disaggregating the population into components.
Wrong (C): Analyzing only year-end balances ignores interim fluctuations and does not describe the concept of data disaggregation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4598;

-- Q4601: Analytical Procedures [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity has complex asset records with frequent additions and disposals","The entity has significant concerns about potential asset impairment charges","The entity frequently changes its depreciation methods across asset classes","The entity has homogeneous assets with consistent methods and reliable data"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 520, substantive analytical procedures are most effective when the auditor can develop a precise and reliable expectation. This is most feasible when the population is large, homogeneous, and predictable—such as a stable asset base with consistent depreciation methods—allowing the auditor to calculate expected depreciation and compare it to the recorded amount.
Wrong (A): Complex records with frequent changes make it difficult to develop a precise expectation, reducing the effectiveness of analytical procedures.
Wrong (B): Significant impairment concerns introduce judgment-intensive estimates that are better tested through detailed substantive procedures.
Wrong (C): Frequent method changes reduce predictability and make it harder to develop a reliable analytical expectation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4601;

-- Q4605: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inventory may be overstated due to obsolete or slow-moving items","Accounts receivable balances may be uncollectible and require write-off","Long-term debt obligations may be misclassified on the balance sheet","The entity is experiencing rapid revenue growth across product lines"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): A decreasing inventory turnover ratio indicates that inventory is selling more slowly relative to its balance, suggesting the presence of obsolete, slow-moving, or excess inventory that may need to be written down to net realizable value. Under AU-C 520, the auditor should investigate whether inventory is overstated and whether appropriate valuation adjustments have been made.
Wrong (B): Accounts receivable collectibility is indicated by the receivables turnover or aging analysis, not the inventory turnover ratio.
Wrong (C): Debt classification issues are unrelated to inventory turnover; they would be identified through analysis of debt covenants and maturity schedules.
Wrong (D): Rapid growth would typically increase sales relative to inventory, causing turnover to increase rather than decrease.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4605;
